WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.567
Your eyes hurt.

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:04.667
The videos stopped being fun twenty minutes ago.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.967
But your thumb is still moving.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.800
Tonight, I’ll show you why

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.900
stopping can feel harder when you’re already tired—

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:14.567
and how to give the scroll an ending.

00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:18.733
Researchers call one version bedtime procrastination:

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.667
going to bed later than intended

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.167
when nothing outside you keeps you up.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:23.467
But

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:27.100
the delay can also happen after you’re already in bed.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.667
Those are not quite the same problem.

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.167
That distinction matters

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:34.500
because the phone is not always keeping you from bed.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.400
Sometimes you are on the sofa,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.667
postponing the moment when the day officially ends.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:42.333
Other times, you made it under the blanket,

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:43.400
turned off the light,

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.000
and brought an endless feed with you.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.200
The first scientific studies

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.967
defined bedtime procrastination narrowly:

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:52.800
you intended to go to bed,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.867
you could have gone to bed, and you delayed anyway.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.567
In one early study of 177 adults,

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:03.000
people who reported more bedtime procrastination

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:05.500
also reported less self-regulation

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.600
and more insufficient sleep.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.833
But that study was correlational.

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:11.867
It could identify a pattern,

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:14.267
not prove one simple cause.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.200
A later exploratory study of 400 teenagers

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:21.433
separated delaying bedtime from delaying sleep

00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:23.000
while already in bed.

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:26.133
The two were only weakly related in that sample.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.900
That does not settle the question for everyone,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.467
but it gives us a useful place to start:

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:33.733
before trying to fix the behavior,

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.933
notice which delay is actually happening.

00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:39.067
Now look at the feed itself.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.033
A chapter ends. A movie reaches its credits.

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:43.633
A friend says goodnight.

00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:45.833
A feed does none of those things.

00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:48.267
Every item arrives with the next item

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:50.300
already waiting underneath it.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.867
The design does not force your thumb to move,

00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:56.333
but it gives your evening no natural final page.

00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:00.033
And by this point, scrolling may not even feel good.

00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:04.067
It may simply be easier than deciding what happens next.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.600
Going to sleep means closing the day.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.633
It means accepting that the free part is over,

00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:11.067
tomorrow is closer,

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:14.333
and anything unfinished will remain unfinished.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.267
This is where the popular phrase revenge

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:20.367
bedtime procrastination can sound persuasive.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.100
Some people do describe

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.467
late-night delay as compensation:

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.700
the day belonged to work, school,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.100
family, errands, or other people,

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.167
so the night becomes the last piece

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:33.067
that feels personally owned.

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:35.800
Emotional regulation and compensation

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.300
have also appeared as possible functions

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.300
in a small behavioral study.

00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:43.100
But possible is the important word.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.800
Not everyone is reclaiming autonomy.

00:02:45.833 --> 00:02:47.800
You may be avoiding tomorrow.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.300
You may be absorbed in a conversation.

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.600
You may have an irregular schedule.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.433
You may be dealing with insomnia

00:02:54.433 --> 00:02:57.633
or another health issue. A useful explanation

00:02:57.633 --> 00:02:59.833
should help you observe the behavior,

00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:02.133
not diagnose you from a thumbnail.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.000
Research on electronic media and sleep

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.933
also needs careful wording.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.400
A systematic review

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.067
covering 55 papers and more than 41,000 participants

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.867
found that heavier electronic-media use

00:03:15.867 --> 00:03:18.733
was associated with poorer sleep outcomes.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.433
But much of this evidence is correlational

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:24.100
and varied substantially between studies.

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.767
The honest conclusion is

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:28.033
not that one screen automatically

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:30.600
destroys sleep. It is that timing,

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:33.167
content, displacement, arousal,

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.967
and habitual use can all matter—

00:03:35.967 --> 00:03:39.100
and reducing the entire problem to blue light

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.967
misses most of the behavior.

00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:45.067
So instead of promising a perfect nighttime routine,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.733
try one bounded experiment for three evenings.

00:03:48.967 --> 00:03:53.067
First, name the delay. Before changing anything,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.967
ask: am I avoiding going to bed,

00:03:55.967 --> 00:03:59.467
or am I already in bed and avoiding sleep?

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.100
Write down the answer in one sentence.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.933
If the answer changes from night to night,

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:07.000
that is useful information.

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:09.833
You are looking for the location of the decision,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.267
not judging yourself for making it.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.333
Second, create an ending before you need one.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.167
Choose a short piece of genuinely wanted free time

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:21.667
earlier in the evening.

00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:24.533
Give it content with a visible boundary:

00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:27.533
one saved video, ten pages,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.300
one episode, one game round,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.100
or a twenty-minute call.

00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:35.267
The activity does not need to be productive.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:39.233
Its job is to feel chosen and to end somewhere.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.733
An endless feed can still be part of that block,

00:04:42.733 --> 00:04:46.000
but decide its endpoint before opening it.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:46.900
A timer

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.300
alone may become another notification to dismiss.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.667
Pair the time with a physical next action:

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.233
when the block ends,

00:04:55.233 --> 00:04:58.500
the phone goes to one specific landing place.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.133
Third, write one if-then sentence.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.467
If my chosen block ends,

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.333
then I place the phone at its landing place

00:05:07.333 --> 00:05:09.733
before choosing anything else.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.900
That sentence does not remove temptation.

00:05:12.967 --> 00:05:15.000
It moves the decision earlier,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.000
before you are lying in the dark

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.300
negotiating with the next video.

00:05:19.633 --> 00:05:23.433
Small behavioral trials suggest that structured plans

00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:27.333
can reduce bedtime procrastination for some people,

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:29.767
but this is still an experiment,

00:05:29.767 --> 00:05:34.300
not treatment and not a guarantee. For three mornings,

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.900
do not ask whether you executed the routine perfectly.

00:05:37.933 --> 00:05:40.333
Ask three narrower questions.

00:05:40.633 --> 00:05:43.600
Did I know which kind of delay was happening?

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.400
Did my free time have a visible ending?

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.767
Did the phone reach its landing place

00:05:48.767 --> 00:05:51.033
before I started negotiating?

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.167
If the answer is no, change the design,

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:56.633
not the insult you use against yourself.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.800
Maybe the free-time block was too late.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.200
Maybe the chosen activity never felt like yours.

00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:06.567
Maybe the landing place was within arm’s reach.

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:10.533
Or maybe the problem is not a habit experiment at all

00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:12.800
and deserves professional attention.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.233
The point is not to make every evening efficient.

00:06:16.333 --> 00:06:20.033
The point is to stop asking your most tired self

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:21.633
to invent an ending

00:06:21.633 --> 00:06:25.033
inside something designed to continue. Tonight,

00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:29.200
before the next scroll begins, decide where it ends.
